Scott: It's just like, yeah, we're going to do all the things with our monsters that definitely aren't Pokemon that you couldn't allow.

205
00:14:25,170 --> 00:14:26,372
Scott: You're not allowed to do with Pokemon.

206
00:14:27,073 --> 00:14:31,761
Scott: And this game is now the number one game on steam, period.

207
00:14:32,161 --> 00:14:37,530
Scott: Counterstrike, Dota, they're behind this game as of now, and it's still early access and it just came out.

208
00:14:37,730 --> 00:14:40,976
Rym: It sold 6 million copies in the first four days it was live.

209
00:14:41,096 --> 00:14:49,109
Rym: And what's weird, I don't think I know a single human who is playing it or wants to play it.

210
00:14:49,850 --> 00:14:51,734
Scott: Well, it sort of became a thing while we were at magfest.

211
00:14:51,754 --> 00:15:01,490
Scott: It's that recent, but the, you can also there, you can play four player co-op, but I think you can also like run your own server to get 32 people together or something.

212
00:15:01,630 --> 00:15:01,910
Scott: I don't know.

213
00:15:03,353 --> 00:15:13,850
Scott: But anyway, the interesting thing about this game is that people have modded in surprise, actual Pokemon right into this game.

214
00:15:14,151 --> 00:15:21,810
Scott: They just, you know, haha, obviously the company can't sell Pokemon, but we can just put Pokemon in there and now it's actual Pikachu with a gun.

215
00:15:21,990 --> 00:15:25,463
Rym: Like in the old days you could take Doom and just put Mickey Mouse in a wad and there he is.

216
00:15:28,010 --> 00:15:30,190
Scott: Well, you could if it was, legally at least.

217
00:15:30,890 --> 00:15:32,217
Rym: I mean, you could do it illegally back then.

218
00:15:32,277 --> 00:15:34,770
Rym: It's not Doom's fault that I made a custom wad with Mickey Mouse.

219
00:15:35,811 --> 00:15:44,230
Scott: So Nintendo, of course, has not failed to notice this, especially since the PALworld company is a Japanese company, the developer and publisher.

220
00:15:44,770 --> 00:15:54,650
Scott: And so the people who are making, they're not going after the PALworld company because, you know, they have differentiated their monsters enough and that they can't be gone after.

221
00:15:54,870 --> 00:16:09,230
Rym: I don't think Nintendo has anything on the PALworld company unless the company enforces- If you're making the official Pokemon mods, Nintendo is going after them unsurprisingly.

222
00:16:09,930 --> 00:16:17,430
Scott: So I suggest A) If you want to do some official Pokemon mods on your PALworld server, get them while they're hot.

223
00:16:17,430 --> 00:16:21,982
Scott: 2) If you're making these mods, stop releasing them publicly.

224
00:16:22,042 --> 00:16:24,230
Scott: Put them on some shady website Nintendo will never find.

225
00:16:26,073 --> 00:16:28,409
Scott: And 3) I don't know if this game is actually any good.

226
00:16:28,670 --> 00:16:38,450
Rym: I read some reviews of this game from people I trust and the reviews all said it's empty and hollow and it feels like the kind of game that NFT people would make.

227
00:16:39,830 --> 00:16:41,090
Scott: Like it's a nothing game.

228
00:16:41,310 --> 00:16:47,870
Scott: I haven't played it even one second, I've just seen video footage, but it seems like it's sort of a cookie cutter kind of deal, right?

229
00:16:47,950 --> 00:16:50,870
Scott: It's sort of trying to beat everything to everybody.

230
00:16:52,730 --> 00:16:56,050
Scott: But hey, at least it doesn't seem like it has microtransactions though, right?

231
00:16:56,891 --> 00:16:57,841
Rym: At least I can tell.

232
00:16:58,750 --> 00:17:09,069
Rym: It's an empty, soulless, according to a bunch of reviewers, like I have not played it, empty soulless open world with some survival and crafting stuff to do if you want.

233
00:17:10,151 --> 00:17:19,869
Scott: It is only $27 in early access, so that's not really unreasonable, especially if there's no microtransaction bullshit and you can run your own server.

234
00:17:20,652 --> 00:17:26,290
Rym: But what's interesting, like the real interesting part about this, is the online reaction.

235
00:17:26,609 --> 00:17:33,570
Rym: There's corners of the internet that the company has said their developers are getting tons of death threats, mostly from people who are...

236
00:17:33,711 --> 00:17:34,629
Scott: I have to read this.

237
00:17:34,750 --> 00:17:35,830
Rym: There's an IGN article about it.

238
00:17:35,890 --> 00:17:39,906
Rym: Palworld devs say they've received death threats amid their Pokemon ripoff claims.

239
00:17:42,470 --> 00:17:43,908
Rym: They've also said they're receiving death threats.

240
00:17:44,070 --> 00:17:48,710
Scott: So I guess there's a handful of Poke Ultra fans probably in Japan.

241
00:17:48,830 --> 00:17:59,710
Rym: Pokemon fans are going after this company, but also people are going after this company claiming that they just used AI to make the art, and they're mad about the fact that AI is involved, which...

242
00:18:00,331 --> 00:18:01,409
Scott: I don't think that that's the case.

243
00:18:02,210 --> 00:18:04,010
Rym: It may or may not be true, but it doesn't matter.

244
00:18:04,230 --> 00:18:05,148
Scott: It's possible, but...

245
00:18:05,250 --> 00:18:11,790
Rym: These character designs are indistinguishable from any generic "I need to make a bunch of monsters" characters.

246
00:18:12,012 --> 00:18:14,390
Rym: The IGN article I read made a good point.

247
00:18:14,690 --> 00:18:24,129
Rym: If Digimon came out today, the people who are sending death threats to this company claiming they're using AI would be sending death threats to Digimon because Digimon looked like they were made by an AI.

248
00:18:25,770 --> 00:18:35,930
Scott: Yeah, I mean, none of these creatures, you know, it's like, yeah, they look like someone made a bunch of, you know, Pokemon imitations that are different enough to avoid lawsuits, right?

249
00:18:36,030 --> 00:18:46,630
Scott: And it's like, but none of them are like particularly inspiring or charming or like you want to have a figure of one of them or, you know, merchandise or anything.

250
00:18:46,890 --> 00:18:51,403
Scott: But I guess people who play this game might become attached to some of these characters, right?

251
00:18:51,504 --> 00:18:53,570
Scott: Even though, you know, who gives a shit?

252
00:18:53,670 --> 00:18:55,613
Rym: Yeah, but it's really fascinating.

253
00:18:55,633 --> 00:19:05,350
Rym: There's tons of these articles talking about people who either are making shit up saying that this company used AI or are trying to prove that they're using AI.

254
00:19:05,831 --> 00:19:12,950
Rym: And it seems like it's almost become a proxy for a subset of the internet for people who are mad about AI in general and this is just the company they're going after.

255
00:19:12,990 --> 00:19:19,110
Scott: Seems like one story that got republished on every single website that, you know, is an AI generated website that republishes news.

256
00:19:19,650 --> 00:19:20,131
Rym: Yeah.

257
00:19:21,554 --> 00:19:30,330
Rym: There's a weird Reddit thread about it too, but I read all these articles and I can't find anything that appears that this company did anything wrong.

258
00:19:30,971 --> 00:19:40,210
Rym: So I feel like what might really just be happening is a new game came out, it's really popular and people seem to get mad whenever something's popular and get weird about it.

259
00:19:41,411 --> 00:19:41,741
Scott: I don't know.

260
00:19:43,070 --> 00:19:48,561
Rym: They're digging out like tweets the CEO made a long time ago talking about, "Hey, what if I used AI to generate

261
00:19:48,581 --> 00:19:49,082
Rym: Pokemon?".

262
00:19:49,442 --> 00:19:53,330
Rym: But those might also themselves be fake tweets and there's no links to them.

263
00:19:53,610 --> 00:19:57,230
Scott: It's the old soldier jumping in front of the whatever meme, right?

264
00:19:57,230 --> 00:19:57,755
Scott: Well, weird nerds.

265
00:20:01,812 --> 00:20:08,248
Scott: In other minor news, right, Polygon has an article about this study, right?

266
00:20:10,490 --> 00:20:13,622
Scott: I can't actually find the data from the study that they're talking about.

267
00:20:13,682 --> 00:20:15,950
Scott: It might have just been like a study they did themselves.

268
00:20:16,430 --> 00:20:21,430
Scott: Polygon surveyed more than 4,000 Americans over the age of 18 about their anime consumption habits.

269
00:20:21,570 --> 00:20:29,289
Scott: So basically this is the study and they haven't published their raw data that I can find, but they did the study themselves.

270
00:20:29,369 --> 00:20:29,610
Scott: Okay.

271
00:20:30,031 --> 00:20:31,749
Scott: And this is just their report on it.

272
00:20:32,511 --> 00:20:40,410
Scott: And basically their study is that, hey, yes indeed what you thought anime is way more popular than it appears to be.

273
00:20:40,510 --> 00:20:47,950
Scott: And you can read all these numbers yourself because obviously we're going to put the link here on our website and in our notes and everything like that.

274
00:20:49,231 --> 00:20:58,490
Rym: You always like to say where on one hand, duh, obviously I knew this, but at the other hand, there's a difference between feeling like you know something and having data to show it.

275
00:20:59,450 --> 00:21:23,510
Scott: Well, I think the thing is, I've been saying this for a while and I'm more sure of it every day, is that there is a huge, I guess, I had the right word for it and I forgot the right word, but there is a huge separation between how popular we think a thing is or how popular a thing is perceived to be and how popular a thing actually is.

276
00:21:23,870 --> 00:21:33,070
Rym: Well, case in point, people who are fans of, I don't know, random generic TV sitcom, it's not like, there might be hundreds of millions of people watching that sitcom.

277
00:21:33,810 --> 00:21:40,990
Rym: How many of them are going to go to a convention about that sitcom or even about sitcoms or even think to mention it to people in most conversations?

278
00:21:41,232 --> 00:21:42,036
Rym: Like it doesn't come up.

279
00:21:42,399 --> 00:21:54,870
Scott: So the image of an anime fan is- There are things which are popular but do not appear to be popular because there is no talk about them either amongst the people or amongst the media, right?

280
00:21:55,070 --> 00:22:06,190
Scott: There are things that are unpopular that appear to be big and popular because like they're in the movie theater and someone's doing a promo circuit and a lot of money is being pumped into it.

281
00:22:06,272 --> 00:22:06,707
Rym: Marvel comic books.

282
00:22:06,810 --> 00:22:12,539
Scott: There are ads and there are, you know, astroturfs and it seems like it's a big deal, right?

283
00:22:12,599 --> 00:22:20,170
Scott: It's like, you know, you could have something where like one person tweets about a thing and then maybe there's five people who are in agreement.

284
00:22:20,771 --> 00:22:30,329
Scott: And then someone does a news story on it and then every news outlet copies that news story and now what was just five people seems like a fad and a trend and then people get in on it.

285
00:22:30,911 --> 00:22:33,468
Scott: But how many people were ever actually in on it, right?

286
00:22:33,852 --> 00:22:35,190
Scott: That kind of thing can happen.

287
00:22:35,350 --> 00:22:36,092
Rym: That whole Fortnite thing.

288
00:22:36,132 --> 00:22:42,850
Rym: Fortnite made a change and if you looked at the internet, you would believe that everyone who plays Fortnite is up in arms and they're going to quit the game.

289
00:22:43,270 --> 00:22:47,690
Rym: And then when all the people who said they quit were quit, Fortnite was like, yeah, that was basically zero people.

290
00:22:48,810 --> 00:22:48,930
Scott: Right.

291
00:22:49,312 --> 00:22:52,442
Scott: So this is- and sometimes it's actually true.

292
00:22:52,543 --> 00:22:54,710
Scott: Think that seems unpopular is unpopular.

293
00:22:54,992 --> 00:22:55,081
Scott: Yeah.

294
00:22:55,630 --> 00:22:58,630
Scott: And, you know, and think that seems popular is popular, right?

295
00:22:58,690 --> 00:23:03,356
Rym: But anime has definitely been- It's just mainstream, generically popular.

296
00:23:03,396 --> 00:23:09,990
Rym: There are definitely tons of people who watch a lot of anime and would never think to even mention that fact to other people.

297
00:23:12,890 --> 00:23:24,870
Scott: So I think that's just something we had to be aware of is the perception of popularity and the truth of popularity are strongly disconnected in many ways.

298
00:23:27,390 --> 00:23:35,090
Rym: In one last little bit of news, this has been making the rounds for a while, but I just wanted to highlight it because this is one example that's very specific.

299
00:23:35,811 --> 00:23:37,417
Rym: There's a board game called Kelp.

300
00:23:37,759 --> 00:23:38,542
Rym: Looks like a cute game.

301
00:23:38,662 --> 00:23:39,827
Rym: I don't have any strong opinion on it.

302
00:23:39,867 --> 00:23:40,530
Rym: I haven't read the rules.

303
00:23:40,590 --> 00:23:41,169
Scott: It was on Kickstarter.

304
00:23:41,450 --> 00:23:42,030
Rym: Yeah, it was Kickstarter.

305
00:23:42,290 --> 00:23:44,030
Scott: I saw it, but I wasn't interested.

306
00:23:44,531 --> 00:23:58,010
Rym: And because it's a small Kickstarter, it is only available via that Kickstarter or the pledge manager related to the Kickstarting on Kickstarter.

307
00:23:58,210 --> 00:24:06,090
Rym: Like, there's nowhere else to buy it unless you go to the secondary market and buy it from someone who already bought it from that pledge manager from the original Kickstarter.

308
00:24:06,090 --> 00:24:08,946
Scott: But the Kickstarter hasn't printed and shipped yet, so you couldn't buy-.

309
00:24:09,690 --> 00:24:10,631
Rym: Well, yeah, we'll get to that.

310
00:24:10,671 --> 00:24:11,072
Rym: That's true.

311
00:24:11,292 --> 00:24:23,750
Rym: So, despite the game having not actually been delivered to people yet, it appeared on Amazon and eBay and tons of other dodgy websites and got really popular.

312
00:24:24,691 --> 00:24:33,670
Rym: And the people who made it put out a press release saying, and I'm quoting, "At the moment, Kelp can only be purchased on our pledge manager.

313
00:24:34,011 --> 00:24:37,886
Rym: We have discovered counterfeit copies of Kelp on platforms like Amazon, eBay, Frugal,

314
00:24:37,906 --> 00:24:38,468
Rym: worldwide.".

315
00:24:38,950 --> 00:24:43,350
Rym: And it goes on to detail everything they know about this situation.

316
00:24:44,050 --> 00:24:45,775
Rym: And this is not the first.

317
00:24:45,976 --> 00:24:47,139
Rym: This is not going to be the last.

318
00:24:47,180 --> 00:24:50,590
Rym: This is going to be a problem that's going to escalate rapidly.

319
00:24:51,470 --> 00:24:57,090
Rym: Because board games like this tend to get printed in a very small number of facilities around the world.

320
00:24:57,951 --> 00:25:10,250
Rym: And it's very easy for anyone involved in that process to just print more or just look at images and recreate a shitty version of the game and then print it at a similar kind of facility.

321
00:25:10,310 --> 00:25:11,494
Rym: There's no checks on this stuff.

322
00:25:11,775 --> 00:25:15,910
Rym: It's not like every factory does a full copyright check of every random job that gets sent to them.

323
00:25:17,551 --> 00:25:20,214
Scott: This has been a problem for decades now.

324
00:25:20,575 --> 00:25:21,956
Scott: And this is the reason the U.S.

325
00:25:21,976 --> 00:25:30,928
Scott: has intellectual property law is because they basically created a regime whereby you can have a job where you designed a thing.

326
00:25:33,110 --> 00:25:36,236
Scott: And you thought of the thing, the knowledge work.

327
00:25:36,717 --> 00:25:38,661
Scott: And you wrote a SOG.

328
00:25:38,841 --> 00:25:43,550
Scott: And that knowledge work gets financially rewarded in capitalism.

329
00:25:44,672 --> 00:25:47,360
Scott: And so when you buy an Apple product, what does it always say?

330
00:25:47,561 --> 00:25:50,570
Scott: Designed in California, manufactured in other country.

331
00:25:52,251 --> 00:25:56,220
Scott: Every single Apple product has a little notice in there that says exactly those words.

332
00:25:56,621 --> 00:26:00,370
Scott: And Apple is reaping most of the profits because they designed it.

333
00:26:00,891 --> 00:26:03,937
Scott: But in the real world, there is no.

334
00:26:03,957 --> 00:26:10,250
Scott: if there were no governments, you know, make enforcing laws that made those things happen on purpose.

335
00:26:10,831 --> 00:26:15,559
Scott: People who design things would see no return on their work.

336
00:26:15,980 --> 00:26:21,950
Scott: Only people who manufacture things are able to see a return because they're the ones who produce the goods.

337
00:26:22,611 --> 00:26:26,296
Rym: The whoever controls the thing.

338
00:26:26,376 --> 00:26:36,110
Rym: that is scarce about something is the only person who can make money without laws, regulations and society backing up the rest of that process.

339
00:26:37,131 --> 00:26:44,530
Scott: Your only hope as a person who designs a thing, a knowledge worker would be to keep your knowledge hidden until the very last minute.

340
00:26:44,630 --> 00:26:48,164
Scott: And you would go to a factory and say, hey, factory, I have a board game.

341
00:26:48,205 --> 00:26:49,550
Scott: It's guaranteed to be a big seller.

342
00:26:49,590 --> 00:26:50,575
Scott: You've seen my other games.

343
00:26:50,595 --> 00:26:51,399
Scott: They were big sellers.

344
00:26:51,780 --> 00:26:53,870
Scott: I will give you the files for this board game.

345
00:26:54,050 --> 00:27:03,090
Scott: They have never been exposed to the outside world except on my secure enclave in exchange for this pile of money, one shot pile of money.

346
00:27:03,170 --> 00:27:10,110
Scott: And then once those files are out, the whole world is now able to produce your game infinitely and you will not be able to see any monies for it.

347
00:27:10,450 --> 00:27:19,010
Scott: That is what it would be like if for people who design things, if there were no governments enforcing any kind of law and capitalism still exists.

348
00:27:19,010 --> 00:27:25,210
Rym: What's specifically happening here is that we've hit a point where this would happen off and on because there's various ways to do it.

349
00:27:25,250 --> 00:27:27,698
Rym: But nowadays, partly because there's print and plays.

350
00:27:28,039 --> 00:27:31,550
Rym: People will post screenshots and videos of a board game before it comes out.

351
00:27:32,630 --> 00:27:36,278
Rym: People can get images or guess at the parts they can't see.

352
00:27:36,799 --> 00:27:41,610
Rym: These aren't even just like the factory, like someone took the factory files and printed more.

353
00:27:42,671 --> 00:27:45,796
Rym: Some of these counterfeits are like badly put together.

354
00:27:45,816 --> 00:27:46,797
Rym: They have errors.

355
00:27:46,837 --> 00:27:55,050
Rym: They were clearly made by hand by people who are dedicated to finding board games they can make black market copies of and just making them.

356
00:27:55,190 --> 00:28:03,010
Rym: Like there's all these screenshots of the absolute garbage that has showed up in the mail of people who bought the game who didn't even think they were pirating.

357
00:28:03,230 --> 00:28:04,881
Rym: They thought they were buying the game legitimately.

358
00:28:04,902 --> 00:28:06,110
Rym: They weren't even trying to pirate.

359
00:28:08,431 --> 00:28:16,190
Scott: We've had lots of counterfeit board games for years now, but this is a new one in that the game isn't even out yet.

360
00:28:16,690 --> 00:28:18,773
Scott: That's what makes this one a different situation.

361
00:28:18,853 --> 00:28:29,090
Scott: Somehow the files were recreated or stolen or hoisted in some fashion, leaked at a factory or stolen from the Kickstarter or somehow.

362
00:28:30,812 --> 00:28:37,490
Scott: Who knows if the counterfeit game is even playable, but they're printing it and selling it regardless.

363
00:28:39,031 --> 00:28:40,153
Rym: But I guess be ready.

364
00:28:40,233 --> 00:28:49,950
Rym: This is the tiniest tip of an iceberg that's about to crash across the entire tabletop world except for those games that have minis for now.

365
00:28:51,113 --> 00:28:57,950
Scott: Well, I think that this is going to scare people who make tabletop games into doing things a bit differently.

366
00:28:58,611 --> 00:28:59,694
Rym: Can't do print and plays.

367
00:28:59,995 --> 00:29:04,910
Rym: You definitely can't let anyone have enough assets to make your game in tabletop simulator.

368
00:29:06,130 --> 00:29:18,450
Scott: Well, that's one person suggested that maybe what happened is since they had like a tabletop simulator demo that someone might have gone and taken the assets from the tabletop simulator and use that to create the counterfeit.

369
00:29:20,252 --> 00:29:34,650
Rym: If a game exists in tabletop simulator, which itself is a dubious application in terms of legality and ethical use of other people's intellectual property, we sidestep all that even if somehow tabletop simulator was super secure.

370
00:29:35,410 --> 00:29:39,070
Rym: Analog whole, someone could play the game, take screenshots and print their shitty counterfeit.

371
00:29:43,020 --> 00:29:46,343
Rym: But anyway, things of the day.

372
00:29:46,483 --> 00:29:54,250
Rym: We actually have two very similar things of the day in that two rare things have happened recently in ice hockey.

373
00:29:55,150 --> 00:29:55,250
Scott: Yep.

374
00:29:55,350 --> 00:29:58,779
Scott: In the game of ice hockey, in the NHL, right?

375
00:29:59,200 --> 00:30:03,470
Scott: Weird things happen in other leagues besides the NHL on a somewhat regular basis.

376
00:30:03,630 --> 00:30:16,250
Scott: I'd say at least a few times a month maybe during hockey season, but in the NHL, if there are things that basically never happen that could theoretically happen and two of them happened on the same day, days apart.

377
00:30:16,992 --> 00:30:20,530
Scott: And it's like, these are extremely, it's like two cocoa beans together.

378
00:30:21,330 --> 00:30:23,196
Rym: Mine is from the end of last year.

