Scott: What'd I

391
00:30:56,828 --> 00:30:57,029
Scott: do?".

392
00:30:57,711 --> 00:31:00,141
Rym: And nobody, like nobody knew this was a rule.

393
00:31:00,242 --> 00:31:01,648
Rym: Like players didn't know it was a rule.

394
00:31:01,668 --> 00:31:02,130
Scott: Refs did.

395
00:31:02,471 --> 00:31:02,771
Scott: Yep.

396
00:31:03,513 --> 00:31:05,979
Rym: It didn't happen again until once in 2006.

397
00:31:05,979 --> 00:31:07,803
Rym: And now in 2023, it happened for a third time.

398
00:31:11,051 --> 00:31:20,650
Rym: And the rule is if you're a goalie in the NHL, you can play the puck anywhere except outside, behind the net in the two corners.

399
00:31:20,790 --> 00:31:26,087
Rym: You can just play the puck, but you can not cross the center line with the puck.

400
00:31:26,147 --> 00:31:27,050
Rym: That is a penalty.

401
00:31:28,232 --> 00:31:33,390
Scott: You can skate over the center line, but you can't touch the puck while you're over the center line.

402
00:31:33,811 --> 00:31:34,509
Rym: You can't control the puck.

403
00:31:34,530 --> 00:31:39,310
Scott: And you can't touch the puck in the back corners outside the trapezoid since they added the trapezoid rule.

404
00:31:39,590 --> 00:31:51,030
Scott: But anywhere else, either in the trapezoid or in your crease or in the zone on your half of the ice or even in the neutral zone on your half of the neutral zone, the goalie can play the puck.

405
00:31:51,451 --> 00:31:57,590
Rym: So when Patrick Wong got the penalty, he was just dicking around and being funny in a game that didn't matter, like they'd already won.

406
00:31:57,970 --> 00:32:01,570
Rym: And he just skates out and he's skating forward with the puck and it was kind of funny.

407
00:32:02,110 --> 00:32:06,270
Rym: But in this case, just watch the video, you'll see how it went down.

408
00:32:06,690 --> 00:32:21,070
Rym: But you might think it's understandable that people wouldn't know about this rule because there is no situation anyone could imagine in the modern NHL where a goalie would on purpose want to be over there where the puck is in play.

409
00:32:22,550 --> 00:32:32,490
Rym: But this is a long-standing rule because in old-timey hockey, the reason this is a rule is because goalies did it all the time and they kept getting hurt.

410
00:32:34,030 --> 00:32:40,228
Rym: And the NHL banned them playing on the other side of the red line to prevent them from getting hurt because they kept doing it.

411
00:32:42,110 --> 00:32:43,289
Rym: So what's your weird hockey thing?

412
00:32:45,630 --> 00:33:02,310
Scott: So in the game of ice hockey, if a team gets a penalty, the ref raises their hand and when the penalized team has control of the puck or basically touches the puck at all, right, they blow the whistle and say, "Hey, we're going to stop playing because we've got to put one of your players in the

413
00:33:02,330 --> 00:33:02,550
Scott: box.".

414
00:33:02,750 --> 00:33:08,000
Scott: But let's say Rym's team gets a penalty and team Scott still has the puck.

415
00:33:08,421 --> 00:33:12,950
Scott: Well, we're going to raise our hand to let you know that a penalty has been called.

416
00:33:13,010 --> 00:33:18,570
Scott: But we're not going to stop you from playing because, well, the team that has the puck didn't do anything wrong, right?

417
00:33:18,791 --> 00:33:22,950
Scott: What if they score a goal right now and we don't want to stop them prematurely?

418
00:33:23,010 --> 00:33:33,150
Rym: They got the same rule in the NHL if someone's injured on the ice unless they're grievously injured where they won't stop play until the team that has the puck in the offensive zone no longer has the puck.

419
00:33:34,270 --> 00:33:34,370
Scott: Right.

420
00:33:34,390 --> 00:33:41,069
Scott: It's like, you know, because otherwise, you know, you could just commit a penalty on purpose to stop my team from scoring a goal.

421
00:33:41,551 --> 00:33:42,933
Scott: Oh, what's his name?

422
00:33:43,154 --> 00:33:51,290
Rym: There's that one guy who did that over and over and over again, like 40, 50 years ago, and the NHL made a rule about it right like the next season.

423
00:33:52,131 --> 00:33:52,351
Scott: Right.

424
00:33:52,491 --> 00:33:57,600
Scott: So obviously you can't have that penalizing on purpose to stop play to stop goals.

425
00:33:57,761 --> 00:34:02,990
Scott: So as long as you're not the team that was penalized, if you're touching the puck, you keep playing.

426
00:34:03,291 --> 00:34:08,964
Scott: And you know in that situation that if the other team touches the puck, the whistle will blow immediately.

427
00:34:09,485 --> 00:34:11,310
Scott: So you don't need a goalie anymore.

428
00:34:11,911 --> 00:34:13,373
Scott: So it's called a delayed penalty.

429
00:34:13,574 --> 00:34:21,790
Scott: And when that happens, usually your goalie will get off the ice to bring out another player skater and try to score and try to keep the puck away from the other team.

430
00:34:22,650 --> 00:34:24,373
Scott: And that's a good situation for you.

431
00:34:24,393 --> 00:34:27,060
Scott: You're basically extending your power play with a delayed penalty.

432
00:34:27,982 --> 00:34:29,545
Scott: Now, the situation is.

433
00:34:29,605 --> 00:34:31,850
Scott: it's like, well, your net is empty then.

434
00:34:32,351 --> 00:34:32,591
Scott: Right.

435
00:34:32,831 --> 00:34:34,795
Scott: And it's like, yeah, but how is it going to go?

436
00:34:35,156 --> 00:34:36,178
Scott: We're not in trouble here.

437
00:34:36,197 --> 00:34:40,466
Scott: We're not going to get scored on because if they touch the puck, this play stops immediately.

438
00:34:40,487 --> 00:34:42,210
Scott: They cannot score on that.

439
00:34:42,850 --> 00:34:49,641
Scott: But you can accidentally put the puck in your own net in that situation, in which case it would be a goal.

440
00:34:50,402 --> 00:34:52,005
Scott: And I've known that.

441
00:34:52,186 --> 00:34:53,007
Scott: Everyone knows that.

442
00:34:53,087 --> 00:34:54,590
Scott: That's not an unknown mystery.

443
00:34:54,730 --> 00:35:01,287
Rym: It is the most common cause of own goals in the NHL is this exact situation.

444
00:35:01,327 --> 00:35:02,590
Rym: But own goals aren't that common.

445
00:35:03,470 --> 00:35:05,274
Scott: But it almost never happens, basically.

446
00:35:05,314 --> 00:35:06,376
Scott: Like, I don't remember it.

447
00:35:06,657 --> 00:35:07,639
Scott: I'm sure it did happen.

448
00:35:07,679 --> 00:35:08,721
Scott: This isn't the first time.

449
00:35:09,242 --> 00:35:11,046
Scott: But I don't remember it ever happening.

450
00:35:11,146 --> 00:35:12,810
Scott: I have never seen it with my own eyes.

451
00:35:12,830 --> 00:35:13,611
Rym: When I was a kid.

452
00:35:14,472 --> 00:35:17,176
Scott: I have never seen it with my own eyes at an NHL game.

453
00:35:18,718 --> 00:35:19,800
Scott: And of course it happened.

454
00:35:19,820 --> 00:35:25,869
Scott: The Penguins embarrassingly lost big with an own goal themselves in exactly this fashion.

455
00:35:25,889 --> 00:35:26,890
Scott: It was disgraceful.

456
00:35:27,870 --> 00:35:28,733
Rym: It's so funny.

457
00:35:29,375 --> 00:35:30,418
Scott: And it's hilarious.

458
00:35:32,143 --> 00:35:33,187
Scott: Just score on yourself.

459
00:35:33,788 --> 00:35:34,270
Scott: Congrats.

460
00:35:35,411 --> 00:35:38,699
Rym: In the moment, the GeekNights Book Club book is the night of short walk on girl.

461
00:35:38,839 --> 00:35:43,150
Rym: I imagine we will do this Book Club episode mid-February.

462
00:35:44,070 --> 00:35:45,650
Scott: Alright, I have to start reading that again.

463
00:35:45,950 --> 00:35:47,132
Rym: Yeah, but I'm going to go on a trip next week.

464
00:35:47,213 --> 00:35:47,974
Rym: And it's a ski trip.

465
00:35:48,014 --> 00:35:49,818
Rym: So there's one, no GeekNights next week.

466
00:35:50,159 --> 00:35:55,850
Rym: But two, I will have a lot of downtime to quietly read before I fall asleep between skiing.

467
00:35:55,950 --> 00:35:56,933
Rym: Because I'm the kind of skier.

468
00:35:57,294 --> 00:35:59,441
Rym: I'm on the first chair and I'm on the last chair.

469
00:35:59,482 --> 00:36:01,929
Rym: And I'm basically skiing as much as possible the entire time.

470
00:36:02,471 --> 00:36:04,894
Scott: Well, if you're traveling to, you know, I don't ski.

471
00:36:05,015 --> 00:36:08,700
Scott: But if I was going to travel to ski, then yeah, I would ski.

472
00:36:09,161 --> 00:36:14,750
Scott: If I'm paying this flat rate for unlimited ski, I'm going to get every ounce of ski that I can get.

473
00:36:15,431 --> 00:36:16,613
Rym: That would be what we would do.

474
00:36:16,673 --> 00:36:21,565
Rym: And it's always weird to me how most skiers, the vast majority of all skiers are not like that.

475
00:36:21,625 --> 00:36:23,690
Scott: They will pay the same price as me.

476
00:36:24,470 --> 00:36:26,176
Rym: The lift opens at like 8.30 in the morning.

477
00:36:26,257 --> 00:36:28,003
Rym: They'll roll in at like 11.

478
00:36:28,003 --> 00:36:30,010
Rym: They'll do a couple runs and then they'll go to the bar.

479
00:36:30,951 --> 00:36:35,810
Scott: Well, maybe they're skiing so much that they're there so many days that they don't need to, you know.

480
00:36:35,890 --> 00:36:37,494
Rym: True.

481
00:36:37,514 --> 00:36:41,264
Rym: I can't just live, well I guess I could just quit my job and go live in Colorado and ski the rest of my life.

482
00:36:41,284 --> 00:36:43,310
Rym: I could actually afford that if I was careful.

483
00:36:45,251 --> 00:36:46,276
Rym: I guess I wouldn't have healthcare.

484
00:36:46,357 --> 00:36:48,226
Rym: so I really would have to make sure I didn't hurt myself.

485
00:36:48,246 --> 00:36:48,929
Rym: But anyway, I digress.

486
00:36:49,090 --> 00:36:55,024
Scott: Like if I went on a trip to some foreign country and I was there for one day, I would wake up at the crack of dawn and do everything I possibly could.

487
00:36:55,044 --> 00:36:57,450
Scott: And if I'm there for a week, I'm going to go to sleep at a normal time.

488
00:36:57,450 --> 00:37:03,844
Rym: That's why I have so many photos of Istanbul at night because the first time I went there, I wasn't sure if I'd ever get back.

489
00:37:03,864 --> 00:37:06,890
Rym: so I was like, I'm going to spend every waking hour exploring the city.

490
00:37:06,970 --> 00:37:10,929
Rym: And I stayed up till like 2 in the morning wandering around every single night the whole time I was there.

491
00:37:11,810 --> 00:37:12,146
Scott: That's right.

492
00:37:13,570 --> 00:37:15,535
Rym: Otherwise, we were just at MAGFest.

493
00:37:15,676 --> 00:37:16,799
Rym: It was fucking awesome.

494
00:37:17,120 --> 00:37:17,862
Rym: Cons are back.

495
00:37:18,062 --> 00:37:20,930
Rym: We're probably going to talk about MAGFest on Thursday because there's a lot to say.

496
00:37:21,391 --> 00:37:25,885
Rym: This MAGFest was, I think, the best run and best MAGFest I've ever attended.

497
00:37:26,467 --> 00:37:27,570
Rym: At least since they went to the Yaylord.

498
00:37:28,110 --> 00:37:31,144
Scott: You know, I'm always worried about MAGFest but nope.

499
00:37:31,646 --> 00:37:32,410
Scott: Nothing to worry about.

500
00:37:32,590 --> 00:37:39,844
Rym: I'd say for the first time in my life, I'm going to say that MAGFest has a higher chance of surviving than it did its previous iteration.

501
00:37:39,864 --> 00:37:42,930
Rym: Like MAGFest is on an upward trend in a big way.

502
00:37:43,842 --> 00:37:44,230
Scott: It is up.

503
00:37:44,623 --> 00:37:44,789
Rym: Yep.

504
00:37:46,790 --> 00:37:47,572
Rym: PAX East coming up.

505
00:37:48,574 --> 00:37:49,155
Rym: We'll be there.

506
00:37:49,616 --> 00:37:51,439
Rym: Probably in Forsen doing some panels.

507
00:37:51,519 --> 00:37:52,581
Rym: TBD and all the details.

508
00:37:52,621 --> 00:37:56,890
Rym: but if you want to see us at PAX East, we will almost definitely be there doing our normal thing.

509
00:37:58,471 --> 00:37:59,395
Rym: What else we got going on?

510
00:37:59,415 --> 00:38:02,105
Rym: I guess PAX West will be that long out.

511
00:38:02,185 --> 00:38:03,310
Rym: Panels will open soon enough.

512
00:38:04,090 --> 00:38:04,791
Rym: So I guess we'll get into it.

513
00:38:04,831 --> 00:38:06,875
Rym: We're not going to talk about MAGFest.

514
00:38:07,216 --> 00:38:11,104
Rym: We're just talking about the games that were at MAGFest.

515
00:38:11,164 --> 00:38:14,592
Scott: Primarily in the... No.

516
00:38:15,174 --> 00:38:18,750
Rym: Ones that were notable or that we noticed or played in some fashion.

517
00:38:19,550 --> 00:38:22,459
Scott: And we're not going to talk about ones that we played in the arcade.

518
00:38:22,479 --> 00:38:25,508
Scott: We're not going to talk about, you know, Adam's Family Pinball Machine.

519
00:38:25,548 --> 00:38:26,090
Scott: It's like, come on.

520
00:38:27,012 --> 00:38:28,761
Scott: Yeah, like I played a bunch of DDR.

521
00:38:28,922 --> 00:38:29,646
Rym: It's DDR.

522
00:38:29,847 --> 00:38:30,330
Rym: Okay, great.

523
00:38:30,370 --> 00:38:30,571
Rym: Right.

524
00:38:30,852 --> 00:38:33,580
Scott: We're talking about a specific section of MAGFest.

525
00:38:33,680 --> 00:38:34,382
Scott: The MIVS.

526
00:38:34,443 --> 00:38:36,830
Scott: The MAGFest Indie Video Game Showcase.

527
00:38:37,011 --> 00:38:39,842
Scott: And this room has not a new room in MAGFest.

528
00:38:39,903 --> 00:38:41,730
Scott: It has been there for years at the MAGFest.

529
00:38:42,870 --> 00:38:52,490
Scott: But in this incarnation of MAGFest 2024, this room was what it had everything that it had the last time I was at MAGFest three or four years ago.

530
00:38:53,151 --> 00:38:59,788
Scott: And it had somehow enhanced greatly into like basically the best room in all of MAGFest.

531
00:38:59,868 --> 00:39:00,510
Scott: And what it was...

532
00:39:01,250 --> 00:39:04,830
Rym: Yeah, MAGFest Indie Arcade Area is basically a combination...

533
00:39:04,830 --> 00:39:07,557
Scott: Well, there's the Indie Arcade and there's the Indie Video Game Showcase.

534
00:39:07,577 --> 00:39:10,445
Scott: There's sort of like these two... It's just one room, right?

535
00:39:10,685 --> 00:39:12,470
Scott: But it's all the same thing to me.

536
00:39:12,790 --> 00:39:24,970
Rym: It is a wide margin better than the best indie setups PAX has ever had in terms of what is there, the quality, the content, the variety.

537
00:39:25,950 --> 00:39:31,144
Rym: MAGFest Indie Video Game Showcase is a very important thing for games and art in my opinion.

538
00:39:31,184 --> 00:39:33,210
Rym: Like I was blown away by what I saw there.

539
00:39:33,590 --> 00:39:35,489
Rym: And I feel like it's just going to get bigger every year.

540
00:39:36,492 --> 00:39:42,124
Scott: Yeah, so I think what it is is that the barrier to entry to get in there is so much lower, right?

541
00:39:42,224 --> 00:39:45,070
Scott: And the culture of going in there is so much different.

542
00:39:45,930 --> 00:39:48,070
Scott: And there's no industry presence whatsoever.

543
00:39:49,130 --> 00:39:50,956
Rym: And the culture has been around forever.

544
00:39:51,076 --> 00:39:55,570
Rym: Remember that Dodecahedron weird looking mirror game that we posted?

545
00:39:55,570 --> 00:39:55,928
Scott: It's still there.

546
00:39:56,130 --> 00:39:56,752
Rym: It is still there.

547
00:39:57,112 --> 00:39:59,116
Rym: That thing's been at MAGFest forever.

548
00:39:59,216 --> 00:40:06,290
Rym: Just like there's the metal skateboards on the janky DDR pad and there's that game where they try to make it like a DDR but on a skateboard.

549
00:40:06,510 --> 00:40:07,371
Rym: That's been there forever.

550
00:40:07,451 --> 00:40:29,804
Rym: But that area historically, nerds who had a weird idea would basically just make... Like this pile of parts or a weird box with a button and they would just interact with it.

551
00:40:30,245 --> 00:40:32,430
Rym: It's literally the kind of thing you'd see in a weird museum.

552
00:40:33,110 --> 00:40:42,648
Rym: And I credit not just MAGFest for making the space, I credit the handful of people who have been bringing their elaborate nonsense to MAGFest for the last 8 or 9 years.

553
00:40:43,810 --> 00:40:48,650
Rym: Just keeping this space warm for the creative explosion that has appeared this year.

554
00:40:49,912 --> 00:40:52,930
Scott: It's definitely a room with a demo scene vibe going on.

555
00:40:53,671 --> 00:41:00,150
Scott: And compared to at PAX, what you have at PAX is there's plenty of indie games there in the expo area and otherwise.

556
00:41:00,390 --> 00:41:07,470
Scott: But those are all supported by these mid tier publishers or the indie mega booth.

557
00:41:09,352 --> 00:41:10,957
Scott: There used to be the indie mini booth.

558
00:41:11,318 --> 00:41:13,144
Scott: This is a lot like the indie mini booth.

559
00:41:13,425 --> 00:41:14,950
Scott: Only no mini booth.

560
00:41:15,170 --> 00:41:18,023
Scott: It's like all the indie mini games get a bigger presence.

561
00:41:20,710 --> 00:41:27,467
Rym: But also at PAX, every game in a place like that is on Steam or available.

562
00:41:27,527 --> 00:41:28,650
Rym: You'll be able to buy it here.

563
00:41:28,770 --> 00:41:39,990
Rym: It's a mix of games that are or will be for sale through traditional channels, but also games that really just like there's one and someone just made it and there aren't more.

564
00:41:40,350 --> 00:41:41,429
Rym: You can't buy it on Steam.

565
00:41:42,610 --> 00:41:43,573
Scott: Yeah, I think that's the other thing.

566
00:41:43,614 --> 00:41:49,070
Scott: If you're going to PAX, it's like you have a game you're trying to sell for money at some point or are already selling.

567
00:41:49,230 --> 00:41:56,230
Scott: Whereas here, some people just make a game, you know, the kind of game that they would throw on itch.io and not ever care to make money off of.

568
00:41:56,390 --> 00:42:04,070
Scott: But they'll come and set it up at Magfest to be played with no expectation of it being for sale ever or anything like that.

569
00:42:05,290 --> 00:42:07,840
Rym: So like to give some examples, there's one game that was there.

570
00:42:08,001 --> 00:42:10,350
Rym: I didn't get a chance to play it, but I watched some other people play it.

571
00:42:10,891 --> 00:42:17,410
Rym: And it's basically a Monty Python silly walk slash upper class twit simulator.

572
00:42:17,951 --> 00:42:24,927
Rym: But the control is a giant bowler hat hanging from the ceiling that you put on your head and try to balance.

573
00:42:24,987 --> 00:42:26,290
Rym: And that's how you control your twit.

574
00:42:26,490 --> 00:42:29,336
Scott: Well, two people wear the hat simultaneously, right?

575
00:42:29,416 --> 00:42:35,690
Scott: So it's two humans with a giant hat that is suspended from like a trestle of some kind.

576
00:42:35,850 --> 00:42:36,091
Scott: Right.

577
00:42:36,532 --> 00:42:42,550
Scott: And two humans strap their heads into this hat and they give you a skullcap because otherwise it'd be gross.

578
00:42:43,571 --> 00:42:49,830
Scott: And then you have to cooperatively use this hat to control what is happening on the screen.

579
00:42:50,731 --> 00:42:56,770
Scott: The line to play the game was long, but I don't feel like I needed to play it to understand what was happening when I looked at it.

580
00:42:56,770 --> 00:43:00,063
Rym: I saw 10 seconds of gameplay and yeah, I'm glad that was there.

581
00:43:00,164 --> 00:43:01,489
Rym: That's a cool thing that someone made.

582
00:43:02,931 --> 00:43:05,529
Scott: Now that's not a thing that anyone's going to be like selling, right?

583
00:43:05,670 --> 00:43:12,030
Rym: Yeah, you're not going to buy the big hat game on Steam and then like take up half the room in your house just to put the big hat.

584
00:43:14,052 --> 00:43:16,830
Scott: It's the kind of thing you could put in like a museum as an installation.

585
00:43:17,330 --> 00:43:18,213
Rym: Yeah, it's exactly that.

586
00:43:18,233 --> 00:43:23,670
Rym: That's why we keep saying these are hybrids of games and installation art.

587
00:43:24,171 --> 00:43:25,477
Rym: And there's a movement like this.

588
00:43:25,517 --> 00:43:28,270
Rym: There is a movement here that is centered around Magfest.

589
00:43:29,411 --> 00:43:34,046
Scott: Now there are some games that could be installations, but some that you could just game.

590
00:43:34,487 --> 00:43:35,270
Scott: Some you can even get.

591
00:43:35,570 --> 00:43:38,820
Rym: Like one that I played that I'm pretty into.

592
00:43:38,840 --> 00:43:42,250
Rym: You know there's that weird Zelda 2 remake where someone just like redesigned the game.

593
00:43:42,790 --> 00:43:44,496
Rym: This one is actually going to be for sale.

594
00:43:45,059 --> 00:43:48,110
Rym: It is the Transylvania Adventure of Simon Quest.

595
00:43:49,510 --> 00:43:52,550
Rym: And it's basically the same as that Zelda 2 situation.

596
00:43:52,730 --> 00:44:03,550
Rym: Someone just took the essence of Castlevania 2 Simon's Quest, but re-implemented it better and avoided compromising intellectual properties so they could actually sell it.

597
00:44:04,230 --> 00:44:18,750
Rym: And I wasn't able to play it until about 2.30 in the morning one night when for the first time in the entire con, the church pew that they had set up in front of the coffins, the real coffins that they had in their little booth.

598
00:44:19,231 --> 00:44:21,758
Rym: Props to them for getting a real church pew and coffins.

599
00:44:22,541 --> 00:44:25,910
Rym: Did not have anyone sitting at it and I played it for like an hour and it was great.

600
00:44:26,390 --> 00:44:28,249
Rym: And I'm definitely going to buy it and play it for real.

601
00:44:31,911 --> 00:44:33,073
Scott: There was an NES game.

602
00:44:33,454 --> 00:44:36,399
Scott: It was like a game that someone actually made for the NES.

603
00:44:36,559 --> 00:44:41,828
Scott: There's a whole section actually in there of games that people have made for old consoles.

604
00:44:43,833 --> 00:44:45,518
Scott: That run on those old consoles.

605
00:44:45,538 --> 00:44:49,050
Scott: There's nothing stopping you from making a new game that's an NES game.

606
00:44:50,590 --> 00:44:54,690
Scott: But obviously are new and not from the olden days.

607
00:44:55,270 --> 00:45:03,527
Scott: And it was just this cute little game where you pick a character and your character has some kind of melee weapon, sort of like a knife.

608
00:45:03,607 --> 00:45:04,108
Scott: it looked like.

609
00:45:04,148 --> 00:45:04,850
Scott: I couldn't really tell.

610
00:45:06,170 --> 00:45:09,496
Scott: But at the bottom of the screen is like popcorn.

611
00:45:10,438 --> 00:45:11,420
Scott: Like a popcorn bag.

612
00:45:11,961 --> 00:45:16,370
Scott: And all these popcorn kernel guys come and try to attack you.

613
00:45:17,231 --> 00:45:21,623
Scott: And if you are touching them, then your health bar is decreasing.

614
00:45:21,723 --> 00:45:23,889
Scott: And when you're not touching them, your health bar goes up.

615
00:45:24,750 --> 00:45:27,980
Scott: If you have your weapon out, your health bar goes down.

616
00:45:28,281 --> 00:45:31,210
Scott: And if you keep your weapon sheathed, your health bar goes up.

617
00:45:31,270 --> 00:45:33,919
Scott: So you can't just run around holding your weapon out all the time.

618
00:45:33,959 --> 00:45:36,085
Scott: You have to strategically stab quickly.

619
00:45:36,126 --> 00:45:37,390
Scott: Like stab, stab, stab.

620
00:45:38,171 --> 00:45:41,964
Scott: And when you stab these guys, they pop into popcorn and fall to the bottom.

621
00:45:42,807 --> 00:45:43,810
Scott: Some of them are blinking.

622
00:45:44,451 --> 00:45:53,770
Scott: If you pop a blinking guy, then he'll turn into a popcorn and any bad guy that popcorn touches on its way down will form a chain reaction of popping.

623
00:45:55,072 --> 00:45:56,034
Scott: And you get a bunch of points.

624
00:45:56,835 --> 00:45:57,496
Scott: That's the whole game.

625
00:45:58,879 --> 00:46:05,330
Scott: The game was surprisingly clever and interesting given the simple concept.

626
00:46:06,810 --> 00:46:10,097
Scott: I just kept playing it even though there wasn't more going on.

627
00:46:10,838 --> 00:46:17,130
Scott: I found an easy strategy to rack up a high score going back and forth off the edges of the screen Pac-Man style.

628
00:46:17,650 --> 00:46:19,596
Scott: Because the bad guys just come right at you.

629
00:46:19,777 --> 00:46:21,161
Scott: They don't have any sort of intelligence.

630
00:46:21,663 --> 00:46:23,328
Scott: So I'm on the left side of the screen.

631
00:46:23,348 --> 00:46:24,030
Scott: They all run left.

632
00:46:24,250 --> 00:46:25,613
Scott: I switch to the right side of the screen.

633
00:46:25,633 --> 00:46:28,099
Scott: They all turn around and come the other way.

634
00:46:28,119 --> 00:46:32,890
Scott: And then eventually I pop one guy who makes a chain reaction and sort of clear things out so it's safe.

635
00:46:33,611 --> 00:46:36,096
Scott: And then I just kept doing that for a long time.

636
00:46:37,058 --> 00:46:39,984
Scott: And then eventually the pressure did mount enough.

637
00:46:40,044 --> 00:46:42,930
Scott: I thought it would sort of cap out and I would be able to play forever.

638
00:46:43,552 --> 00:46:49,608
Scott: But eventually I did, I guess through lack of endurance or increasing number of bad guys, I died.

639
00:46:49,888 --> 00:46:50,430
Scott: And that was that.

640
00:46:51,771 --> 00:46:56,321
Scott: The person who made the game was there and we discussed and recognized that fact.

641
00:46:56,782 --> 00:46:57,905
Scott: And I said, "You know what you should do?

642
00:46:58,767 --> 00:46:59,990
Scott: You should make the popcorn go

643
00:47:00,110 --> 00:47:00,170
Scott: up.".

644
00:47:00,791 --> 00:47:07,065
Scott: I thought the popcorn was going to go up and create sort of a Bomberman-like pressure where the arena shrinks.

645
00:47:08,648 --> 00:47:09,550
Scott: But that never happened.

646
00:47:10,071 --> 00:47:12,599
Scott: And I was like, I was expecting this to happen and it didn't happen.

647
00:47:12,619 --> 00:47:13,021
Scott: And they said,

648
00:47:13,041 --> 00:47:14,625
Scott: "Uhhh.".

649
00:47:15,408 --> 00:47:16,010
Scott: And that was that.

650
00:47:16,750 --> 00:47:19,516
Scott: And I'm sad that I can't remember the name of the game.

651
00:47:19,676 --> 00:47:24,246
Scott: I took a photo of my high score and didn't take a photo of the game.

652
00:47:25,388 --> 00:47:26,230
Scott: Of what game it was.

653
00:47:26,510 --> 00:47:29,378
Rym: A game that we played together and I played several times.

654
00:47:29,658 --> 00:47:34,090
Rym: It actually was made by a former coworker of Emily's because they both worked at the same game studio.

655
00:47:34,770 --> 00:47:35,972
Rym: Nothing good can come of this.

656
00:47:35,993 --> 00:47:46,030
Rym: It has some big Nidhogg vibes in that it's one of those extremely tight, like knife fight in a phone booth kind of symmetric direct competition games.

657
00:47:46,250 --> 00:47:48,250
Scott: It's a gun fight in a phone booth to be more precise.

658
00:47:48,290 --> 00:47:48,972
Rym: In this case true.

659
00:47:49,353 --> 00:47:53,483
Rym: So basically you've got two little guys standing in a very small space and there's a hole in the ceiling.

660
00:47:53,824 --> 00:47:55,688
Rym: And you can punch a little bit and you can jump.

661
00:47:55,708 --> 00:47:56,109
Rym: and that's it.

662
00:47:56,591 --> 00:47:59,156
Rym: You punch each other, just knock each other back a little bit and you can jump.

663
00:47:59,877 --> 00:48:02,402
Rym: And after a few seconds, a gun falls.

664
00:48:03,724 --> 00:48:06,530
Rym: And then a few more seconds later, a bullet falls.

665
00:48:07,250 --> 00:48:13,510
Rym: And you can only get a point by holding the gun and the bullet at the same time and successfully shooting the other person.

666
00:48:14,490 --> 00:48:16,353
Scott: But you try to shoot the other person.

667
00:48:17,075 --> 00:48:25,430
Scott: If they're too close to you, the gun will actually be behind them and it's like you'll be holding the gun and using their shoulder as an armrest and shoot behind them.

668
00:48:25,811 --> 00:48:30,021
Scott: You have to be far enough away from them that the gun is gonna actually hit them.

669
00:48:30,201 --> 00:48:31,384
Scott: And that is not so easy.

670
00:48:34,170 --> 00:48:35,355
Rym: But if you're holding the gun, you can't punch.

671
00:48:35,375 --> 00:48:38,950
Rym: So if you're holding the gun, the other person's got the bullet, you gotta throw the gun and try to hit them.

672
00:48:39,290 --> 00:48:42,019
Rym: But if you mess that up, they might pick up the gun and they already have the bullet.

673
00:48:43,082 --> 00:48:45,550
Rym: It's a really tight game and it just forces you to win.

674
00:48:46,011 --> 00:48:48,235
Rym: You've got to get over the line.

675
00:48:48,576 --> 00:48:50,459
Rym: It's like a really hard, tight duel.

676
00:48:50,499 --> 00:48:55,930
Rym: It feels a lot like Nidhogg and that is a genre similar to Outlaw that I'm just a fan of in general.

677
00:48:56,290 --> 00:48:57,231
Rym: I love that genre.

678
00:48:57,752 --> 00:49:18,407
Rym: But the cool thing is, they had it set up in an arcade machine and part of what has helped this creative explosion is just in the last few years, there are multiple companies and multiple avenues to very easily build, buy, or otherwise craft full-size arcade machines that have arbitrary stuff inside of them.

679
00:49:18,427 --> 00:49:19,630
Rym: Like it's got the barrier entrance.

680
00:49:19,690 --> 00:49:25,780
Scott: All you really gotta do is get some wood and if you have woodworking, you can make the cabinet.

681
00:49:26,160 --> 00:49:32,030
Scott: And then inside the cabinet, all you need to put is a Raspberry Pi and a screen and a power cable.

682
00:49:32,070 --> 00:49:35,820
Scott: And then controls, you can just buy joysticks and buttons anywhere.

683
00:49:35,981 --> 00:49:39,250
Scott: And there you go, you got yourself an arcade cabinet decorated how you would.

684
00:49:40,312 --> 00:49:43,270
Rym: There was a Black Emperor and I did get to play Black Emperor.

685
00:49:43,610 --> 00:49:44,452
Scott: It was the Black Emperor.

686
00:49:44,472 --> 00:49:51,170
Scott: I gotta say Black Emperor, now having played it, if you don't know, it was the Omega-Thon finale at PAX West.

687
00:49:53,731 --> 00:49:55,934
Scott: The controls on it are extremely tight and good.

688
00:49:56,014 --> 00:49:56,896
Scott: Perfect, basically.

689
00:49:57,877 --> 00:50:02,124
Scott: It is not actually a hard game at all, control-wise.

690
00:50:02,164 --> 00:50:04,007
Scott: You can perfectly control that bicycle.

691
00:50:04,027 --> 00:50:06,110
Scott: I got the hang of it in like two seconds, motorcycle.

692
00:50:07,390 --> 00:50:17,410
Scott: The only challenge in that game, it is the challenge of memorization because the things come at you so fast that no human could react to most of the changes.

693
00:50:17,410 --> 00:50:20,254
Scott: Because when sand comes at you, you have to hit the throttle harder.

694
00:50:20,695 --> 00:50:30,970
Scott: And when the road turns, you have to turn and it comes at you so fast, you can't possibly react fast enough to not die no matter where you position yourself on the screen.

695
00:50:31,571 --> 00:50:37,287
Scott: So you have to just memorize what's coming up next because it is set in stone, at least until it changes.

696
00:50:39,310 --> 00:50:44,330
Rym: It's the same game as Kaboom for the most part, just with a radically different style.

697
00:50:44,430 --> 00:50:45,875
Scott: Effectively speaking, yes.

698
00:50:46,317 --> 00:50:48,444
Scott: It's Kaboom, but there's also a button you have to regulate.

699
00:50:48,464 --> 00:50:50,270
Scott: Kaboom, you only have to regulate the wheel.

700
00:50:51,210 --> 00:50:55,190
Rym: Speaking of memorization, so Emily and I played a game called Bunny Beats by Studio Tapioca.

701
00:50:55,892 --> 00:50:56,509
Scott: Oh, I saw that one.

702
00:50:56,650 --> 00:50:59,129
Rym: It's got really cute art, but... It's a rhythm game.

703
00:50:59,431 --> 00:51:02,160
Rym: It's a rhythm game, but it's a rhythm game that doesn't fuck around.

704
00:51:02,441 --> 00:51:03,385
Rym: We played it on Easy.

705
00:51:03,445 --> 00:51:03,706
Rym: It is hard.

706
00:51:05,610 --> 00:51:21,870
Rym: I appreciate it in that it is a rhythm game that someone who is good at music and understands rhythm and possibly is a percussionist would love, but someone who does not have rhythm and is not good at music could not play it.

707
00:51:22,632 --> 00:51:24,016
Rym: It basically is a bunch of bunnies.

708
00:51:24,417 --> 00:51:27,185
Rym: They're making mochis, so they're hammering the mochi.

709
00:51:27,506 --> 00:51:28,890
Rym: It's really cute and there's a bunch of them.

710
00:51:29,350 --> 00:51:34,130
Rym: And they're all playing basically a different part of what is a percussion song.

711
00:51:35,130 --> 00:51:50,530
Rym: And the way the game works, it'll show you one of the bunnies will be highlighted, and you have to pay attention to just that bunny and see what part the bunny is playing, memorize it and learn it, having heard it only once.

712
00:51:51,232 --> 00:51:57,390
Rym: And then, in the next stanza of the music, play that bunny's part without any notes or any prompts or any help.

713
00:51:58,550 --> 00:52:00,374
Scott: Yep, this is the middle challenge, right?

714
00:52:00,434 --> 00:52:03,000
Scott: Because you think, "Oh, I could be a percussionist.

715
00:52:03,060 --> 00:52:03,701
Scott: It's so easy,

716
00:52:03,762 --> 00:52:03,942
Scott: right?".

717
00:52:05,044 --> 00:52:07,530
Scott: Some people cannot keep a beat or a rhythm whatsoever.

718
00:52:07,952 --> 00:52:11,026
Scott: I, at least, believe that I can keep a beat or a rhythm.

719
00:52:11,047 --> 00:52:12,292
Rym: The problem is... I've seen you play DDR.

720
00:52:12,312 --> 00:52:18,030
Rym: You definitely can keep a beat or a rhythm, and you can definitely follow instructions on a screen of when to step where.

721
00:52:18,210 --> 00:52:21,876
Scott: I can keep exactly one beat, a rhythm, right?

722
00:52:22,578 --> 00:52:23,419
Scott: At a time, right?

723
00:52:23,479 --> 00:52:26,484
Scott: I can be like, you know, one, two, one, two, one, two, right?

724
00:52:26,745 --> 00:52:29,950
Scott: To be an actual drummer, you have to be able to have one hand...

725
00:52:29,950 --> 00:52:32,330
Rym: Polyrhythm, three on one hand, four on the other hand simultaneously.

726
00:52:33,310 --> 00:52:38,790
Scott: Right, you have to have one hand doing one rhythm and the other hand doing a completely different rhythm, right?

727
00:52:40,811 --> 00:52:47,306
Scott: Not even one hand going one, two, one, two, and the other hand going one, two, three, four, where they're sort of sharing, right?

728
00:52:47,527 --> 00:52:48,910
Scott: That's still the same rhythm.

729
00:52:49,571 --> 00:52:51,643
Scott: We're talking about completely different.

730
00:52:51,784 --> 00:52:52,629
Scott: One hand is going like...

731
00:52:57,791 --> 00:53:08,230
Rym: But it's not like most rhythm games that even have memorization components where it shows you the rhythm, or it shows you the thing, and then you do the thing, and it's kind of synthetic.

732
00:53:08,571 --> 00:53:15,866
Rym: This is more like you're going to hear someone play something, and then you've got to play the same thing in context, and it's a skill that musicians have.

733
00:53:16,247 --> 00:53:17,590
Rym: I don't think anyone else has that skill.

734
00:53:18,510 --> 00:53:20,633
Scott: Right, this is in this sort of middle area.

735
00:53:20,713 --> 00:53:34,950
Scott: It's not asking you to do the full challenge of two simultaneous different rhythms, but it is asking you to do a rhythm while other rhythms are in your ears that aren't the one you're going to play, right?

736
00:53:35,671 --> 00:53:41,956
Scott: And the one you are going to play is not being hinted at, right, while you are playing it visually.

737
00:53:42,217 --> 00:53:56,910
Scott: You have to have memorized it from having heard it sort of like Simon, but it's like if you were four Simons, and you had to reproduce just one of the Simons, and the other four were beeping, the other ones were beeping away at you, right?

738
00:53:57,310 --> 00:54:00,144
Scott: At the same time, it's like that's what this game is.

739
00:54:00,868 --> 00:54:01,330
Scott: Pretty clever.

740
00:54:01,550 --> 00:54:13,470
Rym: But the reason why it's not just impossible is that you're playing the rhythm in the context of a legitimate real song, so it'd be like someone who's an artist- Your rhythm does fit with the other ones.

741
00:54:14,091 --> 00:54:21,026
Rym: Yeah, and picking up on how it fits and what it's doing, which, again, I don't think anyone who is not a musician is capable of.

742
00:54:22,088 --> 00:54:23,010
Rym: That's the challenge.

743
00:54:25,591 --> 00:54:34,650
Scott: Another game that I saw and played, which is available on itch.io at blairzang.itch.io/bbb, is called Bamboom Blade.

744
00:54:35,051 --> 00:54:36,550
Rym: Oh, that's real good.

745
00:54:37,510 --> 00:54:38,311
Scott: This is real simple.

746
00:54:38,372 --> 00:54:48,450
Scott: So basically, it's trying to simulate, you know, kung fu movie wuxia sword fight, you know, situations, sort of like Crouching Tiger Hidden Dragon style.

747
00:54:49,091 --> 00:55:01,630
Scott: There are two very tall bamboo trunks, and on each trunk, there is a warrior person with a sword and nothing else, and they're holding on to that bamboo trunk.

748
00:55:02,391 --> 00:55:03,975
Scott: And the controls are very simple.

749
00:55:04,056 --> 00:55:04,838
Scott: It's twin stick.

750
00:55:05,440 --> 00:55:08,450
Scott: The left stick allows you to move up and down the trunk.

751
00:55:09,311 --> 00:55:19,350
Scott: And the second stick- well, that same- the left stick also, if you move left or right, will allow you to bend the bamboo trunk to the left or right, basically shifting your weight.

752
00:55:19,851 --> 00:55:22,760
Scott: So you can whip yourself around on this trunk like,

753
00:55:22,780 --> 00:55:24,245
Scott: "Ooh-wee!".

754
00:55:24,988 --> 00:55:25,670
Scott: It's a 2D game.

755
00:55:26,891 --> 00:55:29,879
Scott: The right stick only controls your sword.

756
00:55:29,959 --> 00:55:32,005
Scott: If it aims up, you'll hold your sword in the sky.

757
00:55:32,025 --> 00:55:34,030
Scott: If you aim it down, you'll put your sword down.

758
00:55:34,090 --> 00:55:42,610
Scott: If you spin the stick around in a circle, you'll spin your stick- sword around like a, you know, whirlwind, like a- like a fan that's gonna chew up something that gets thrown into it, right?

759
00:55:44,311 --> 00:55:51,990
Scott: And so, there is someone else on the other stick with the same controls, and you are trying to stab them to death without being stabbed to death.

760
00:55:53,391 --> 00:56:00,370
Scott: The rules of this game are quite fascinating in that I found it wasn't quite Bushido Blade, where any touch of the sword kills someone.

761
00:56:01,091 --> 00:56:05,338
Scott: It's very smart with locations and physics and speed.

762
00:56:05,759 --> 00:56:12,350
Scott: Like, for example, in one game, I came together with my opponent, my sword went right into their leg.

763
00:56:12,671 --> 00:56:14,675
Scott: Like, right into it, and their blood was coming out.

764
00:56:14,716 --> 00:56:15,758
Scott: Gary- I knew I hit him.

765
00:56:16,299 --> 00:56:19,266
Scott: Their knee hit me in the head, and I died.

766
00:56:19,727 --> 00:56:20,810
Scott: The sword did not touch me.

767
00:56:22,610 --> 00:56:24,333
Scott: So, that's the kind of game it is.

768
00:56:24,353 --> 00:56:42,390
Scott: If you want to have a two-player fighting game that has a really low barrier to entry, but really sort of high skill cap and possibilities, it's obviously not gonna take you as high as an actual fighting game, like a Street Fighter kind of situation, but you can get a lot of play out of this one.

769
00:56:42,410 --> 00:56:45,276
Scott: And I think you can even get more play out of this than, like, a dive kick.

770
00:56:47,120 --> 00:56:48,724
Scott: So, I appreciated it very much.

771
00:56:48,744 --> 00:56:51,390
Scott: I don't even think they're asking any money.

772
00:56:52,330 --> 00:56:56,846
Scott: One that stood out- Yeah, I pushed download, and it didn't even ask me for money.

773
00:56:56,886 --> 00:56:57,990
Scott: It just, like, starts downloading.

774
00:56:58,530 --> 00:56:59,275
Rym: We'll get in on that.

775
00:56:59,315 --> 00:57:03,191
Rym: That had to be in Outlaw 2 for us, Nidhogg 2.

776
00:57:03,191 --> 00:57:07,370
Scott: It's very similar to Outlaw, just a one-on-one, completely symmetric situation.

777
00:57:07,711 --> 00:57:11,602
Rym: So, I forget the exact title of this game, but it's, like, Bad Art Forgeries.

778
00:57:11,622 --> 00:57:13,988
Rym: It was a really cute little thing.

779
00:57:14,008 --> 00:57:14,650
Rym: It was just set up.

780
00:57:14,770 --> 00:57:20,610
Rym: And basically, you get in the middle of the screen, there's a pixel representation of a famous painting.

781
00:57:21,510 --> 00:57:24,054
Rym: And it's a versus art forgery game.

782
00:57:24,074 --> 00:57:36,050
Rym: You have a very limited amount of time to, with a mouse and a very coarse MS paint set of brushes, recreate that painting as fast as possible.

783
00:57:36,431 --> 00:57:41,224
Rym: The winner is whoever gets the most pixels correct by the end of the timer.

784
00:57:41,826 --> 00:57:42,307
Rym: I won.

785
00:57:42,328 --> 00:57:43,210
Rym: I beat Emily.

786
00:57:44,150 --> 00:57:45,915
Rym: Our paintings look very different from each other.

787
00:57:46,757 --> 00:57:48,140
Rym: My percentage was 62.3.

788
00:57:48,140 --> 00:57:49,202
Rym: I got 62.3% of the pixels correct.

789
00:57:52,830 --> 00:57:55,199
Rym: And Emily got 61.92% of the pixels correct.

790
00:57:58,790 --> 00:58:01,923
Scott: It's literally counting how many pixels are the correct color.

791
00:58:02,506 --> 00:58:03,470
Scott: That's all the game is.

792
00:58:03,711 --> 00:58:04,515
Rym: It's great.

793
00:58:04,616 --> 00:58:07,210
Rym: It is real fun, and it's a really clever idea.

794
00:58:08,290 --> 00:58:14,930
Scott: Okay, the absolute best game that I saw that I don't want to miss is a game called QuestFast, a customer copy adventure.

795
00:58:15,070 --> 00:58:18,390
Rym: Oh my god, that was one of the most impressive things in that whole space.

796
00:58:18,450 --> 00:58:19,413
Scott: It is the best one.

797
00:58:19,594 --> 00:58:24,470
Scott: So, this game, game-wise, is just an adventure game, right?

798
00:58:24,871 --> 00:58:30,610
Scott: Like a text adventure, or a Queen's Quest, or a Monkey Island, or a Shadow Gate, right?

799
00:58:30,650 --> 00:58:36,168
Scott: Where it's like, look at the room, go north, use this item on that thing.

800
00:58:36,208 --> 00:58:36,910
Scott: That's the game.

801
00:58:37,430 --> 00:58:39,557
Scott: There's nothing special about it in that regard.

802
00:58:39,978 --> 00:58:43,950
Scott: It's basically a text adventure where you're going to make breakfast, right?

803
00:58:44,030 --> 00:58:50,795
Scott: Get eggs, get milk, crack eggs into bowl, turn on stove, put eggs in pan- You're trying to make breakfast.

804
00:58:51,197 --> 00:58:52,001
Scott: That's the whole game.

805
00:58:52,463 --> 00:58:53,830
Scott: Nothing else to it, right?

806
00:58:54,653 --> 00:58:58,770
Scott: What makes this game special is the interface for the game.

807
00:58:59,590 --> 00:59:14,910
Scott: They created an entire cash register with a 10-key numpad, a little LCD display with two rows of text, two receipt printers, and a barcode scanner, as well as some markers and a little bulletin board you can use.

808
00:59:15,591 --> 00:59:24,270
Scott: And what happens is there's one receipt printer that is the main one, and that one is basically printing this never-ending long receipt.

809
00:59:24,971 --> 00:59:29,390
Scott: And that never-ending long receipt is actually the main output of the game.

810
00:59:29,970 --> 00:59:42,965
Scott: It says, like, if you tell it, "Look at the plate," then it prints, and you'll see the plate, and you'll see a description of the plate, and it confirms, "The action you chose was to look at plate.

811
00:59:43,447 --> 00:59:43,989
Scott: Here's what you

812
00:59:44,049 --> 00:59:44,230
Scott: saw.".

813
00:59:44,732 --> 00:59:47,621
Scott: And that's the output, right?

814
00:59:48,183 --> 00:59:50,390
Scott: There's also a side receipt printer.

815
00:59:50,831 --> 01:00:04,010
Scott: Whenever you acquire an item, it prints out a tiny little receipt and cuts it, and you get a little slip of paper that has that item and also a barcode on it that represents that item.

816
01:00:04,670 --> 01:00:08,229
Scott: There's also, I think, like, seven barcodes that are, like, always just there.

817
01:00:08,870 --> 01:00:13,670
Scott: Like, those are for the main actions, like, you know, look, take, eat, et cetera.

818
01:00:14,091 --> 01:00:23,615
Scott: So you would scan on the look barcode, which is always there, and scan the plate, the little tiny receipt you're holding, and that's how you look at the plate.

819
01:00:23,675 --> 01:00:28,610
Scott: You scan -- everything you do in this game is always scanning two barcodes.

820
01:00:29,090 --> 01:00:30,938
Scott: Scan an action, and then scan a thing.

821
01:00:31,219 --> 01:00:32,625
Scott: Scan an action, scan a thing.

822
01:00:32,665 --> 01:00:33,910
Scott: That's all the input for the game.

823
01:00:34,571 --> 01:00:46,890
Scott: And the most genius thing is that on that main, the main receipt printer, the big long one that's going infinitely, that has all the output from the game, when it prints the room you're in, right?

824
01:00:47,912 --> 01:00:50,729
Scott: It actually prints out a little picture of the room you're in.

825
01:00:51,431 --> 01:00:57,070
Scott: And if you look closely at it, you'll notice all the items in the room have barcodes on them.

826
01:00:57,170 --> 01:01:07,270
Scott: So it's like when you open the fridge, you'll see there's, like, eggs in the fridge, and it's a picture of eggs, but the picture of the egg carton is filled in with a barcode.

827
01:01:07,611 --> 01:01:18,802
Scott: So you click "look" -- well, you click, you scan the look barcode, and then you go over to the big receipt, the mega long one, and you scan the eggs in there, right?

828
01:01:19,364 --> 01:01:21,049
Scott: And it's like, "Ah, yes, you see a carton of

829
01:01:21,149 --> 01:01:21,330
Scott: eggs.".

830
01:01:21,891 --> 01:01:28,110
Scott: And then you scan "take eggs," and then the tiny receipt printer prints out a little receipt with the eggs.

831
01:01:28,610 --> 01:01:36,010
Scott: And so that way, you can go to a different room and still have the egg barcode available now to do something else with.

832
01:01:36,931 --> 01:01:50,020
Scott: That's the whole game, but what shocked me the most, not only did they have this genius input system for a basic adventure game, but that it was functional and responsive and fast.

833
01:01:50,180 --> 01:01:54,850
Scott: Like, you scanned, and it printed out quickly in shocking resolution.

834
01:01:55,150 --> 01:02:01,548
Scott: It didn't run out of paper, it didn't run out of ink, maybe I just got to it when it was working well, but it was working so well.

835
01:02:02,691 --> 01:02:06,744
Scott: Like, it was probably might even be faster than Shadowgate on the NES, right?

836
01:02:06,804 --> 01:02:08,589
Scott: It's like beep beep, right?

837
01:02:09,970 --> 01:02:11,539
Scott: You were going fast in this game.

838
01:02:11,559 --> 01:02:13,429
Scott: It was an impressive game.

839
01:02:13,810 --> 01:02:15,544
Rym: Just what an installation.

840
01:02:15,645 --> 01:02:16,270
Rym: that set up for us.

841
01:02:16,270 --> 01:02:28,550
Scott: It was made by Team Registration at WPI IMGD Questfast, designed and developed by Eddie, Garrett, Xian, Zihang Chen, and Lexi McFann in just over a week.

842
01:02:29,871 --> 01:02:31,278
Scott: Took them over a week to make this.

843
01:02:31,761 --> 01:02:33,429
Scott: Some genius kids over there at WPI.

844
01:02:34,892 --> 01:02:40,605
Rym: So, I think we've gone on long enough, but suffice it to say, that was only a small sampling.

845
01:02:40,645 --> 01:02:41,146
Scott: I got more.

846
01:02:41,186 --> 01:02:42,730
Scott: There were genius things happening.

847
01:02:43,010 --> 01:02:48,369
Rym: I mean, I was looking at the website, and there were games I see on the website that I did not notice, despite trying to explore that area.

848
01:02:48,730 --> 01:02:53,050
Scott: I know that all of these I saw, but not all of them were genius.

849
01:02:53,130 --> 01:02:57,489
Rym: I didn't even get to talk about the book ritual, or Hernah, or Tug of Peace, which I loved.

850
01:02:57,610 --> 01:02:58,557
Scott: I didn't get to talk about Dr.

851
01:02:58,617 --> 01:03:00,430
Rym: Garf, who I fucked.

852
01:03:00,770 --> 01:03:03,630
Rym: Or South Scrimshaw text adventure with a giant whale.

853
01:03:04,851 --> 01:03:06,135
Scott: Yeah.

854
01:03:06,657 --> 01:03:10,250
Scott: But yeah, there was just so much going on in that room.

855
01:03:10,831 --> 01:03:17,670
Scott: That room was at previous MAGFest, but it was twice or at least twice as stacked as it was previously.

856
01:03:18,091 --> 01:03:20,370
Scott: I could have spent way more hours in just that one room.

857
01:03:20,931 --> 01:03:26,585
Rym: It was notable how expanded and amazing that room was.

858
01:03:26,645 --> 01:03:27,848
Rym: I cannot praise it enough.

859
01:03:27,928 --> 01:03:28,810
Rym: Good job, MAGFest.

860
01:03:29,410 --> 01:03:33,810
Rym: I think Mibs is going to grow and be a very, very critical part of that con forever.

861
01:03:34,050 --> 01:03:41,490
Scott: The way I see it is, if you look at anime cons, they got their dealer's room, and then they got their artist alley, which is really just cheap dealer's room.

862
01:03:41,890 --> 01:03:43,630
Rym: This is more like installation alley.

863
01:03:44,591 --> 01:03:46,436
Scott: Right, but that's only half of it.

864
01:03:46,476 --> 01:03:47,800
Scott: The other half of it is just booths.

865
01:03:47,860 --> 01:03:49,705
Scott: Normal old booths, not installations.

866
01:03:49,745 --> 01:03:51,310
Scott: Only the arcade part is installations.

867
01:03:53,010 --> 01:03:57,759
Scott: But it's like if there was a second PAX expo hall.

868
01:03:58,019 --> 01:04:01,205
Scott: that was super low rent, and you couldn't get in there.

869
01:04:01,265 --> 01:04:03,329
Scott: if you were a real company, it wouldn't let you.

870
01:04:05,951 --> 01:04:10,909
Scott: But it's just like the cost to get in there was so low, and the culture of MAGFest is being what it is.

871
01:04:11,952 --> 01:04:15,250
Rym: Speaking of the cost to get in there, do you know how that room actually works?

872
01:04:17,197 --> 01:04:18,030
Scott: Do you have to apply?

873
01:04:18,590 --> 01:04:26,130
Rym: No, so all the people who want to put something in that room, they submit a video of uninterrupted gameplay between 30 seconds and 2 minutes.

874
01:04:26,610 --> 01:04:38,990
Rym: The judges watch the video and decide if your game is accepted, you are given the space, you are given power and tables, you are given free badges, and you are given the right to buy a hotel room in the Gaylord.

875
01:04:40,574 --> 01:04:43,049
Scott: Oh, that makes me want to make a game just to get the hotel room.

876
01:04:48,430 --> 01:04:50,799
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

